          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I get short of breath climbing two flights of stairs but I am completely fine at rest.",
          "answer": "**Urgency: routine, based on the fixed red-flag rules.** Breathlessness that appears only on exertion and clears at rest did not match any emergency pattern in the rule table. That is not a clearance. Exertional breathlessness can have serious causes, so it is worth a non-urgent appointment soon, not something to ignore.\n\n**Seek emergency care right away if any of these happen** (from the severe breathing difficulty guidance):\n- Breathlessness at rest, or breathlessness that starts suddenly.\n- You can't speak in full sentences, or you have to pause for breath between words.\n- Blue or gray lips, tongue, face or fingertips.\n- Chest pain along with breathlessness.\n- Drowsiness, confusion or exhaustion while struggling to breathe.\n\n**Get medical advice promptly** if the breathlessness is getting worse, is new, or is coming on with less effort over time.\n\n**Possible causes, grouped by body system (not a ranked list, and not a diagnosis):**\n- **Heart and blood vessels:** The coronary artery disease guidance notes that breathlessness on exertion can be a presentation of heart disease even without chest discomfort. This is one reason a clinician should look at it rather than leave it.\n- **Lungs and airways:** The COPD and asthma guidance describe breathlessness that varies with activity and triggers. Both need assessment, and asthma symptoms often come and go.\n- **Muscles and joints:** The osteoarthritis guidance describes joint pain that is worse with activity. Joint pain could limit how you climb stairs, but that guidance does not describe joint problems causing breathlessness, so this is less clear.\n\n**Before your appointment**, note when it started, how many flights trigger it, how quickly it clears, and whether you have any chest discomfort, cough, wheeze or other symptoms. Your clinician can decide which tests, if any, are needed.\n\nI could not complete a further search for more detail, so this answer relies only on the passages retrieved above.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
